hich is all about  automation in artificial intelligence  so how is canada positioned for this  massive shift  [Music]  when sean gaffari became associate dean  of applied technology at humber college  in 2018  he saw the future of automation in  canada at a crossroads  the the stake is very very high here  because we are talking about the global  competition  and a lot of companies countries in the  world  they are ahead of us he looked at the  programs in canadian schools and saw a  problem  students were ill-prepared for this new  wave and canadian companies he believes  weren't going to invest in the future  if there were a few people qualified to  take them there we just need to  act fast we just need to  connect all of these thoughts together  to finally address  this skill gap which exists in the  industry  today there are 24 colleges in ontario  alone offering automation programs  along with mechatronics programs at four  universities  that growth has attracted top students  to the province including mauricio toygo  who says it's not hard to quantify the  opportunity  the brazilian expat says even during a  pandemic  graduates are finding jobs fast  you have not only to invest in in  developing  and implementing automation in our  factories but  we also need people that are capable of  working  uh with those machines that are capable  of developing them so the investment on  edu on the education side  and the industry side has to go um you  know at the same time  what's the risk if we don't get this  right the risk is that we're going to  lose our manufacturing capabilities and  we're going to have to  pretty much import everything that we  want to use and that's  not really good for for canada as a  whole  [Music]  the highly automated factory is still  far in the future for most manufacturers  digital advancements and ai have changed  jobs that were traditionally thought to  be impossible to be automated  but it's happening think driverless cars  automated buses and even  computer-generated social media  influencers  all this leads to the question can  humans in automation  coexist most of what's going to happen  is that robots are going to  complement people not replace them  outright but the end result is still not  so clear  university of waterloo economics  professor joel blitt decided to take a  long hard look at what canada needed to  do  post-pandemic automation he concluded  was both  inevitable and necessary to better  compete with the rest of the world  it's just common sense that taking days  to fulfill a curbside pickup order  can't compete with amazon's next day  delivery  if canadian companies take the plunge  into automation  blitz predicts a hollowing out of the  middle class  but the thing i'm most worried about is  potentially increasing inequality  because even after these you know the  market settles down  and people find new jobs it's not the  case that their new jobs are going to be  as good as their old jobs  history supports his argument as other  technological revolutions took hold  it led to a recalibration of the  workforce  in his book the technology trap carl  frey writes it took  decades for the lower class to recover  but if the same thing were to happen  today  canada with its vast social safety net  isn't a bad place to be  the places that seem to be doing better  are  the places that also have you know  reasonable welfare  systems like sweden so if you don't lose  your health care  with your job you have less to worry  about  from its perspective sobes refuses to  see automation  as a job killer will this result in  fewer jobs or have you had to hire  people  so it is absolutely the opposite it is a  job creation and i believe that  investments in innovation and  and in technology as i said they are not  without  people you need the people as well and  they are i think there's a perception  one way but the truth in our case is  we're creating 1500 jobs  it's a point automation consul